"The surah begins with the expression, \"I swear by this city.\" What is meant by this city is the Sacred City (al-Balad al-Haram), namely Mecca the Honored (Makkah al-Mukarramah). Commentators are in consensus on this matter. Naming Mecca as \"al-Balad\" indicates that it is a blessed, well-known city worthy of reverence. Allah swore by this city, and subsequently by the father and the son, to emphasize that man was created amidst hardship.\n\nThe phrase \"While you are free of restriction in this city\" addresses the Prophet (peace be upon him). The word \"hill\" carries meanings such as being lawful, leaving the state of ihram, or settling in a place. Here, it indicates that fighting in Mecca was made lawful for the Prophet for a brief period. Indeed, on the day of the conquest, permission was granted to kill a few individuals, after which the sacred inviolability of Mecca continued. This demonstrates the virtue of the Prophet and the honor of that city.\n\nThere are various interpretations regarding the expression \"I swear by the father and his children\" mentioned in the third ayah. According to one view, the father (walid) is Adam (peace be upon him), and what he fathered (ma walad) is his offspring. According to another view, Abraham (peace be upon him), his son Ishmael, and his descendant Muhammad (peace be upon him) are intended. The most comprehensive interpretation is that every father and his child are included. This oath draws attention to the importance of the human lineage and the purpose of creation.\n\nThe ayah stated as the response to the oaths, \"Indeed, We created man in hardship and toil,\" explains that human life is full of difficulties. The word \"kabad\" derives from the word for liver and means hardship and fatigue. From the mother's womb until death, man faces various hardships. These difficulties are for the trial of worldly life and preparation for the hereafter.\n\nThen, it is narrated that man, presuming himself strong, says, \"I have squandered heaps of wealth.\" This refers to arrogant individuals who waste their wealth or spend it out of hostility toward the Prophet. Allah asks that person: \"Does he think that no one has seen him?\" That is to say, Allah has seen everything he did and will call him to account.\n\nAllah reminds of the blessings He has bestowed upon man: two eyes, a tongue, and two lips. These meet man's basic needs such as seeing, speaking, and eating. Then it is stated, \"And We showed him the two paths.\" These two paths are the path of good and the path of evil. Man has been given the ability to do both good and evil, and the choice of which one to take has been left to him.\n\nThe arrogant man could not attempt that steep ascent. This steep ascent, called \"al-Aqabah\", refers to demanding deeds of goodness. Overcoming this steep ascent is to free a slave, or to feed an orphan or a destitute person on a day of famine. These are deeds that require great sacrifice and heroism.\n\nThose who believe and counsel patience and compassion are the people of happiness; their books will be given from their right side. As for the disbelievers who deny Our verses, they are the people of ill omen; they will be cast into the Fire in Hell, and the Fire will be closed in over them. This symbolizes their eternal punishment."